; A %{exe:...} reference to a target of this project, in the directory that
; builds it, so it carries no path to be recognised by. It reaches E941 beside
; %{bin:...} as "gen.exe", and the suffix is what says it is this project's own
; target rather than a binary some package installs: no (public_name ...) ends
; in .exe. Here to hold that. Were the suffix dropped, the name would read as a
; demand for an installed binary called "gen", nothing would provide it, and a
; scan narrowed to this package -- which cannot see whatever might provide it
; -- would report a rule it could not evaluate and exit 2, where the package is
; in fact clean. Every package with a fuzz/ directory has this shape.
(executable
 (name gen))

(rule
 (alias check)
 (deps %{exe:gen.exe})
 (action
  (progn)))

; A demand for a binary no package in this workspace installs. Narrowed to
; pkg-a, the index answers nothing for it -- and that IS the answer, the same
; one the whole workspace gives, because no package left out of this scan
; installs a binary of that name either. Were the rule to treat every
; unresolved binary under a narrowed scan as undecided, this package would
; stop being clean below.
(rule
 (alias check)
 (deps %{bin:no-such-tool-anywhere})
 (action
  (progn)))
